This paper addresses the challenge of identifying $k$ target items from a large pool of candidates using a budget of $n$ queries. Unlike prior research that focused on single-target search, we examine the scenario where multiple targets must be discovered. We introduce a novel algorithm that achieves a constant approximation ratio for $k=2$ and a ratio of $O(\log k)$ when $k$ is large. Furthermore, we establish a lower bound of $\Omega(\log k)$ for any algorithm operating under this model. Our findings indicate that the budget limitation significantly restricts the performance of existing state-of-the-art multi-target search methods. Additionally, we explore the relationship between our proposed model and the well-known $k$-arm bandit problem. Finally, we validate our theoretical results through experiments on real-world datasets. The source code is accessible at \url{https://github.com/ChenYi2019/BLIS}.

Keywords: Interactive Search, Multiple Targets, Approximation Algorithm.

\textbf{Introduction}
Interactive search is a fundamental task in numerous real-world applications, including medical diagnosis, legal case analysis, and fraud detection. In these contexts, identifying the target item is crucial for subsequent actions. However, due to resource constraints, practitioners often face a limited number of queries. Consequently, the primary objective is to minimize the count of queries required to locate the target. This study investigates the problem of searching for $k$ target items within a large candidate pool under a query budget of $n$. We refer to this as the Budget Limited Interactive Search (BLIS) problem.

\textbf{Related Work}
The BLIS problem is closely linked to the $k$-arm bandit problem, a classic framework in reinforcement learning. In this setting, an agent selects one of $k$ arms to pull, receiving a random reward, with the goal of maximizing total rewards over time. This problem has been extensively studied, yielding various algorithms and theoretical insights. Notably, Lai and Robbins (1985) proved that any algorithm achieving a regret of $o(kn)$ is suboptimal, and they introduced the Upper Confidence Bound (UCB) algorithm, which is asymptotically optimal. Subsequent research has refined these results, with Li et al. (2010) demonstrating that UCB achieves a regret of $O(kn\log n)$.

